examples.question_text:
Amira and Noah share counters in the ratio $4:1$. Amira receives $18$ more counters than Noah. How many counters does Noah receive?

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.9cm,y=1cm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\foreach \x in {0,1,2,3} {\draw[black] (\x,1) rectangle +(1,0.6);}
\draw[black] (0,0) rectangle (1,0.6);
\path (-0.15,1.3) node[left] {Amira};
\path (-0.15,0.3) node[left] {Noah};
\draw[black,dashed] (1,1.65)--(1,-0.15);
\draw[black,<->] (1,-0.35)--(4,-0.35);
\path (2.5,-0.35) node[below] {$18$ counters};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
The gap represents $4-1=3$ parts.

$\begin{align*}3\text{ parts}&=18\text{ counters}\\1\text{ part}&=18\div3=6\text{ counters}\end{align*}$

Noah receives $6$ counters.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.9cm,y=1cm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\definecolor{answerblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\foreach \x in {0,1,2,3} {\draw[black] (\x,1) rectangle +(1,0.6);\path (\x+0.5,1.3) node[text=answerblue] {$6$};}
\draw[black] (0,0) rectangle (1,0.6);
\path (0.5,0.3) node[text=answerblue] {$6$};
\path (-0.15,1.3) node[left] {Amira};
\path (-0.15,0.3) node[left] {Noah};
\draw[black,dashed] (1,1.65)--(1,-0.15);
\draw[black,<->] (1,-0.35)--(4,-0.35);
\path (2.5,-0.35) node[below] {$18$ counters};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
